WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:00.900
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Is your company

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.680
equipped to deal with software
development and delivery

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.840
as it adopts technologies
like containers, hybrid cloud,

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.790
or even multi-cloud?

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:09.360
Yes?

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:09.960
No?

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:10.860
Maybe?

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.100
Well, keep watching as we unwrap
the modern CI/CD with Anthos.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:20.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:23.700
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and welcome to Anthos 101.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.420
In this episode, we're talking
about all things CI/CD.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.430
On your journey to
container adoption

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.130
across hybrid and
multi-cloud environments,

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.990
it is important
to think seriously

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:37.080
about continuous integration
and continuous delivery.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.330
Oftentimes you will
see operations teams

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.230
getting bigger.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.350
Security best practices
tougher to enforce over

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.290
more environments,
and reliability issues

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.420
making their way
into production.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:52.590
So let's unwrap hybrid
and multi-cloud continuous

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.690
integration and
continuous delivery,

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:58.680
an approach that can help you
exhilarate your release cycles

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.530
and increase your software's
reliability across all

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:02.910
your environments.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:06.960
Modern CI/CD on Anthos
embraces a shift left approach

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.200
to development and leverages
declarative templates

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.240
to manage security
policy, not people.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.710
This means that issues are
addressed during build and test

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.900
phase, not in production.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.080
It allows you to bring your
operations teams together,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.640
cutting down on silos and
the proliferation of tools.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.130
So let's dive in.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.220
When we think about the
creation of a CI/CD pipeline,

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.720
there are three
different teams that

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.780
are involved, developers,
operators and security

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:37.650
engineers.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:40.560
In this model, developers
have their own GIT repository

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.680
where they store their
application code.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:46.080
That application code can kick
off continuous integration

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.960
whenever a developer
creates a commit, a change

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.040
to their application code.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.940
A continuous integration
tool tests the code

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.690
and then creates an artifact.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.690
When we're talking about
deploying to Kubernetes

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.390
clusters, our artifacts
are container images

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.660
that will eventually be
pushed to production.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.060
Once continuous integration
creates that container image,

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.630
it will store it in
container registry.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.060
The reference to the
recently stored image

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.390
will be stored in another
GIT repository controlled

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:18.150
by your application
operations team.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.720
We'll call this the
deployment repo.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.780
This secondary repository
holds Kubernetes manifests,

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.180
which have instructions
on how applications

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.950
should be configured.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.470
In order to manage
that configuration,

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.140
we use a tool called Customize.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.890
Customize packages together
the desired state container

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.990
image from our
container registry

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.980
and the instructions
from the Kubernetes

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:46.210
manifest, and store it in
the deployment repository.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.570
This GIT repository
stores the state

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.960
that we want to deploy
into our environments.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.630
The first place where
we store that state

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:58.440
is within the staging branch
of the deployment repository.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.950
From that staging branch,
a continuous delivery tool

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:05.220
manages the deployment to
our staging environment,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.310
where developers can
test this application.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.590
With Anthos's
consistent platform,

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.070
the staging environments are
more similar to your production

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:15.250
environments.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:18.730
Think about problems created
with different tools, practices

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:20.880
and processes when
testing in the cloud

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.220
and then pushing to
production in a totally

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.030
different environment.

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:27.480
When the developers
have finished testing,

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.430
they can tell the
operators that they

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.650
want to make a
push to production,

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:34.470
or the operators can
control the timing.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:37.770
To push to production, they
march from the staging branch

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:41.280
of the deployment repository
into the master branch

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.210
and propagate those changes
into production environment.

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.220
For our security
teams, let's talk

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.340
a minute about how to secure
your production environments.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.590
You also have your
own GIT repository

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.530
that is going to store the
expected state of the security

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:58.170
policies, things like
role-based access,

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:01.350
control policies, quotas
for your applications

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.170
and restricting risky
behavior, such as the ability

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.840
for containers to run as root.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.350
How can you do that?

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:12.160
Well, one mechanism we use
is the policy controller,

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.270
which is a part of
Anthos Config Management.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.370
Because of Anthos
consistent platform

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:19.920
across all of your
environments, security teams

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.340
can customize what policies
they want to run in what groups

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:24.460
of clusters.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.910
And this is all done without
having to configure it

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:28.800
in each environment.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.710
That was a quick
overview of modern CI/CD

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.860
on Anthos, showing how you
can increase your development

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.130
velocity and produce safe,
reliable applications

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.110
for your customers.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:41.260
Ready to get started?

